# Figma version history — checkpoints for the consumer file

Named versions are the studio's undo for irreversible Figma writes. {{SYSTEM_NAME}} work mutates a SHARED
consumer file (`{{CONSUMER_FIGMA_KEY}}`), and the rebind swap especially can't be cleanly hand-reversed
once instance layout has re-cached and property refs have detached. A checkpoint costs one call, never
touches the canvas (it only adds a labeled restore point), and turns "I hope I can undo this" into a
guaranteed roll-back. Run it on the consumer file. Load `skills/figma-gotchas.md` first when the
surrounding work uses `use_figma`.

> Scope: the CONSUMER file's version history only. The {{SYSTEM_NAME}} SOURCE file's versioning is the DS
> context's concern — never checkpoint or restore `{{DS_FIGMA_KEY}}` from here.

---

## When to checkpoint (the discipline)

| Moment | Who | Label prefix |
|---|---|---|
| Before a screen build | `ui-designer` | `pre-build:` |
| When both Figma gates clear (design-review + binding audit) | `ui-designer` | `gate-pass:` |
| **Before a rebind — the most important one** | `consumer-migrator` | `pre-rebind:` |
| After a {{SYSTEM_NAME}} publish is consumed | rebind / migrator | `consume:` |
| After the usability gate validates a flow | `product-designer` | `milestone:` |

The `pre-rebind:` snapshot is the load-bearing one: `swapComponent` caches new layout, resets vector
fills, and detaches property refs — painful and error-prone to reverse by hand. Snap before the first
swap and the whole migration becomes one-click reversible.

## Naming convention (the label contract)

`<type>: <scope> — <context>`, type ∈ `{pre-build, gate-pass, pre-rebind, consume, milestone}`.

```
pre-build:  the-flow
gate-pass:  the-flow — design-review + binding audit clear
pre-rebind: a-component → vX.Y  (N instances)
consume:    {{SYSTEM_NAME}} vX.Y
milestone:  the-flow validated (SEQ X.X, task success XX%)
```

Keep the type prefix — versions then sort and scan, and a generated changelog reads as a clean timeline.

## Create a checkpoint (via `use_figma`)

Self-contained — set the title + description and run. It adds a restore point; it does **not** modify
any node, so there is nothing to render-verify.

```javascript
// Named version-history checkpoint (run via use_figma)
const v = await figma.saveVersionHistoryAsync(
  "pre-rebind: a-component → vX.Y",                                  // title = the label
  "Snapshot before swapping N instances onto the freshly published main." // description
);
console.log(v.id);   // VersionMetadata.id — record this in your report
```

> **Runtime caveat (verified on a live file).** `saveVersionHistoryAsync` is in the
> Plugin API typings but some `use_figma` runtimes reject it at call time with **"figma.saveVersionHistoryAsync
> is not yet supported"** — the function exists (a `typeof` guard passes) but throws when invoked.
> When that happens, there is **no programmatic create path** here (the REST API has no
> create-version endpoint either). Fall back to a **manual** named save in Figma's UI
> (File → Save to version history / ⌥⌘S) for the checkpoint, and say so plainly. Probe before relying
> on it: guard the call in `try/catch` and report "checkpoint unavailable in this runtime" rather than
> failing the whole task — a checkpoint is a safety net, not the deliverable.

## Read / compare / restore (figma-console MCP)

Reading runs against the REST history, so these work outside `use_figma` — **but require a valid token
with the `file_versions:read` scope** (OAuth) or the *Versions* Read permission (PAT). In practice this
path returns **403 "Token expired"** when the figma-console MCP's token lacks the scope or has lapsed —
if so, the read side is unavailable and you must say the changelog can't be produced this session. The
tools, when authorized:

- **List versions** — `figma_get_file_versions` (id · label · description · created_at · author).
- **Changes since a version** — `figma_get_changes_since_version`.
- **Diff two versions** — `figma_diff_versions`.
- **Changelog between two points** — `figma_generate_changelog`.
- **Blame a node** (who/when last changed it) — `figma_blame_node`.
- **Restore** — identify the target with a diff first, then restore in Figma's version-history panel
  (right-click the version → *Restore this version*). Restore **appends a new version** rather than
  destroying history, so it is itself reversible — there is no destructive overwrite. Programmatic
  node-copy "restores" are not the path; use the panel.

## Integration (who calls this, when)

- **`ui-designer`** — `pre-build:` right after Plan Mode approval, before the first write; `gate-pass:`
  when both delivery gates clear.
- **`consumer-migrator`** — `pre-rebind:` right after Plan Mode approval, before the first swap.
- **After a DS publish lands + is consumed** — `consume:` (pairs with `rebind`).
- **After the usability gate validates a flow** — `milestone:`.

## Report (what to return)

| Checkpoint label | Version id | Created | Trigger |
|---|---|---|---|

For a diff / changelog request, return the changelog grouped by version with the node-level changes,
newest first, and name the two endpoints you compared.

## When to use which surface

- **This skill, inline** — one checkpoint / diff / changelog at a time in the main thread.
- **Folded into `ui-designer` / `consumer-migrator`** — they call it automatically at their write
  boundaries (the wiring), so you rarely invoke it standalone.
- **No agent of its own** — version history is a cross-cutting safety + audit practice, not a pipeline
  role. If a hands-off "audit the history → changelog timeline across files" pass is ever needed, that
  would be a thin read-only agent loading THIS skill — not by default.

## Common traps

- **Checkpointing the {{SYSTEM_NAME}} SOURCE file** — out of scope; that's the DS context.
- **Saving the snapshot AFTER a botched rebind** instead of before — defeats the purpose; the
  pre-write checkpoint is the one that saves you.
- **Treating "restore" as destructive** — it isn't; it appends a version. Still, diff first so you
  restore the right one.
- **Relying on Figma's automatic autosave versions** — they're unnamed and get pruned; named
  checkpoints are what survive and scan.
- **`figma_capture_screenshot` right after writes** can hang the Desktop Bridge — same gotcha as
  elsewhere; checkpoint creation via `saveVersionHistoryAsync` is unaffected (it renders nothing).
- **Read tools live on a different MCP server.** Checkpoint *creation* rides `use_figma`, but the
  list / diff / changelog / blame tools (`figma_get_file_versions`, `figma_diff_versions`,
  `figma_generate_changelog`, `figma_blame_node`) are **figma-console MCP** tools. If that server isn't
  connected this session, the read side is unavailable while checkpointing still works — confirm
  figma-console responds before promising a changelog, and say so plainly if it's absent.
